            <ArticleTitle>Genetic variations of the FCER2 gene and asthma susceptibility in north Indian children: a case-control study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>660-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/1354750X.2013.840802</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The findings showed that the low-affinity IgE receptor plays a pivotal role in allergic immune response and it is a pharmacogenetic predictor in asthma disease. This study aims to investigate the association of genetic variations: rs28364072 and rs7249320 with asthma and its severity in north Indian children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case-control-based genetic association study was performed among 550 children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Statistical analysis demonstrated significant association between asthma and genotypes frequency of both the SNPs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our data indicate that the studied variations are strongly associated with asthma susceptibility and might be risk factor among north Indian asthmatic children.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
